```c
typedef int (*Operation)(int, int);

// Example usage:
int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
Operation op_func = add;
int result = op_func(5, 3); // result is 8
```

```c
typedef int MyInt;
MyInt *ptr; // Correct way to declare a pointer to MyInt
// This is equivalent to: int *ptr;
```
`typedef` creates an alias, not a new distinct type. So `MyInt *ptr;` means "pointer to an `int`", not "pointer to a `MyInt` type distinct from `int`."
